 (roadmap 5.4): `WaterManager.boil_along_beam(points, delta)` boils the WATER of every `boilable` basin a beam polyline crosses, emitting steam at the crossing and draining the basin at its `boil_volume_scale` (see Laser turret); `BOIL_STEAM_VOLUME_PER_SEC`, `BOIL_DRAIN_VOLUME_PER_SEC` and `BOIL_ENTRY_NUDGE` set its rates. `WaterBasin` gains the authored exports `boilable` and `boil_volume_scale`. **Electrified water**: a Spark (see Rail bot) or an Arc (see Arc turret) reaching the settled surface of a crust-free WATER basin (or the top of a frozen crust over one, see Frozen crust) electrifies the whole pool for `electrified_time` (default 1 s, exported; a later strike restarts the countdown, never adds to it). `WaterBasin.try_electrify_at(world_point)` is the only entry (it applies the refusals listed under Rail bot, then sets the runtime `_electrified_left`, kicks a cosmetic ripple through `disturb()`, plays `interactive/basin_zap` at the surface and returns true); `is_electrified()` reads it and `is_hazardous_now()` (a hazardous liquid, or electrified) is the one question the player's swim check and `WaterManager.find_skim_basin()` ask, so a future transient hazard goes there and nowhere else. `conducts_sparks` (default true) and `electrified_time` are authored exports; the countdown is runtime-only and **not saved**, so a restore comes back calm. While electrified the pool is hazardous like slag but only to a submerged player (`ELECTRIC_CONTACT_DAMAGE`, 1 per `take_hit()`, paced by its i-frames, in every held state), and a surfer cannot skim it (no skim floor is raised, so a board over it falls in, ends the surf and is hurt). It affects nothing else: floating blocks, flowing particles, the basin sensor and power circuits do not read it. The look is placeholder code in `_draw_electric()` (flickering tint, bright surface line, zigzag arcs, a bolt at the strike point, tapering over the last `ELECTRIC_FADE_TIME`); `_process` stays on while it runs. **Frozen crust** (the freeze ray, roadmap N3.2): a second **kind** of crust, formed by `WaterBasin.freeze()` instead of the water/slag reaction, recorded in the saved `crust_frozen` flag (`is_frozen_crust()`). `FreezeTurret` hands its beam to `WaterManager.freeze_along_beam(points, delta)`, which, for every basin whose `can_freeze()` holds (the authored `freezable` export on, liquid present, no crust) and whose liquid the beam crosses (the same polygon test as boiling), calls `WaterBasin.add_frost(delta)`; the basin keeps a runtime `_frost` (seconds of beam, decaying at `FROST_DECAY_PER_SEC` once the beam leaves, tracked by physics frame so turret order never matters) and freezes at the authored `freeze_time` (default 2 s). `freeze()` is valid for any crust-free basin that holds liquid, **water or slag, electrified or not** (an electrified pool keeps its remaining time on the crust), and forms the same slab as the reaction crust (top face at the liquid surface, `CRUST_THICKNESS_TILES` thick, the liquid between the faces becomes crust, the liquid under it stays) with no opposite liquid and **no steam**; the cooling animation uses the frozen skin of the basin's liquid (`FROZEN_CRUST_SKIN_PATH_BY_TYPE`: water `sprites/liquids/spsh_freezing.png`, a liquid-blue-to-pale-ice palette; slag `sprites/liquids/spsh_freezing_steel.png`, molten-glow-to-pale-ice; a missing file falls back to that liquid's own crust art, then to the flat `FROZEN_CRUST_COLOR`). A frozen crust is otherwise an ordinary crust (solid, walkable, hangable from beneath over a void, read as `CRUST` by a basin sensor), except that it is **slippery underfoot**: the crust's `CrustBody` answers the player's `is_slippery()` floor contract (true only for a frozen crust, so a reaction crust is ordinary ground) and the player gets the ice-rink acceleration and friction of an ice block (the surf's friction scale is not changed). **Frozen slag is temporary**: `LiquidProfile.frozen_is_temporary` is set for slag only, so the crust body